<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <!--
    Root cause of issue #2011: this repo previously shipped with no committed
    NuGet.Config, so `dotnet restore` relied entirely on whatever ambient
    machine/user-level config a given host already had (e.g. a corporate
    NuGetDefaults.Config that points developer machines at an internal
    package-feed proxy and disables nuget.org by default - see
    https://learn.microsoft.com/nuget/consume-packages/configuring-nuget-behavior#nuget-defaults-file).
    On the affected macOS QA host, that internal proxy
    (https://packagefeedproxy.microsoft.io/nuget/v3/index.json) had not yet
    mirrored OpenTelemetry / OpenTelemetry.Exporter.Console 1.18.0, so restore
    failed with NU1102 even though nuget.org has always had that version.

    We deliberately do NOT reference the internal proxy here: it isn't reachable
    from GitHub-hosted CI runners or other environments outside the corporate
    network, and hardcoding it would break restore everywhere else. Instead we
    just guarantee nuget.org is present and enabled as an additive fallback
    source for every environment that builds this solution. This is the
    documented, supported way to override an org NuGetDefaults.Config that
    disabled a source (per the docs linked above): re-declare the source and
    explicitly set it to enabled in a config closer to the project. It does not
    disable, remove, or otherwise change any org-provided proxy source - hosts
    that already have the proxy configured keep using it for everything the
    proxy actually has.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>
  <disabledPackageSources>
    <!--
      Microsoft's own docs describe re-declaring a source with
      `<add key="nuget.org" value="false" />` here as the standard way to
      override a parent config that disabled it (see the "NuGet defaults
      file" section of the doc linked above). We tested that exact override
      against this NuGet client and it does NOT work: with only
      `<add key="nuget.org" value="false" />` present (no `<clear/>`), a
      forced, cache-bypassing restore of Farm.Web.Api.csproj fails with
      NU1101 ("No packages exist with this id in source(s): ...") for
      OpenTelemetry / OpenTelemetry.Exporter.Console - i.e. nuget.org is
      still excluded from restore even though the override entry is present.
      `<clear/>` is the only mechanism that reliably re-enables nuget.org for
      restore (verified the same way: forced restore succeeds and
      redownloads both packages from nuget.org).

      This does mean any OTHER source an ambient host config disabled is
      also re-enabled here, not just nuget.org - we have no visibility into
      what, if anything, other hosts disable beyond nuget.org, and nothing in
      this repo declares another disabled source today. If a future need
      arises to keep some other specific source disabled for this repo's
      builds, add it back explicitly below the `<clear/>` (e.g.
      `<add key="some-other-source" value="true" />`); collection entries
      declared in this file are honored normally, only inherited ones are
      dropped by `<clear/>`.
    -->
    <clear />
  </disabledPackageSources>
</configuration>
